The "500 Internal Server Error" is simply the server's way of crashing or aborting the evaluation once it detects this violation.

Why this error occurs
In this speedrun, the benchmarking harness (the code that measures how fast your solution is) provides a specific hipStream_t to your function. It uses this stream to record start and end events to calculate your "speed."

If you launch work on a different stream (such as the default stream 0 or a stream you created yourself):

Measurement Error: The harness will "finish" timing as soon as the provided stream is empty, even if your kernels are still running on another stream. This would make your code look artificially fast (a common "cheat" in GPU benchmarks).

Rule Violation: To ensure fairness and accurate profiling, the competition requires all operations to happen within the stream provided by the environment.

How to fix it
To resolve this and avoid disqualification, you must ensure that every asynchronous HIP/ROCm call in your code uses the stream handle passed to your function.

Explicitly pass the stream: If your entry point looks like void my_kernel_wrapper(..., hipStream_t stream), make sure you pass that stream variable into all your HIP calls:

C++
// WRONG (uses default stream 0)
hipLaunchKernelGGL(my_kernel, blocks, threads, 0, 0, args...); 

// CORRECT (uses the provided stream)
hipLaunchKernelGGL(my_kernel, blocks, threads, 0, stream, args...);
Check memory transfers: Ensure all memory operations are asynchronous and tied to the same stream:

C++
// WRONG
hipMemcpyAsync(dst, src, size, kind, 0); 

// CORRECT
hipMemcpyAsync(dst, src, size, kind, stream);
Remove stream creation: Do not use hipStreamCreate() or hipStreamCreateWithFlags() within your submission code. The competition environment expects a single-stream execution.

Library Streams: If you are using libraries like rocBLAS or rocFFT, you must set the stream on the library handle:

C++
rocblas_set_stream(handle, stream);

